1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,904
Back in Section two, you learned the core mechanism: chunk, embed, index, retrieve.

2
00:00:04,904 --> 00:00:09,184
That diagram was deliberately minimal, enough to understand what vector search

3
00:00:09,184 --> 00:00:09,952
actually does.

4
00:00:09,952 --> 00:00:14,561
A real production pipeline has two stages that diagram left out entirely, one at the

5
00:00:14,561 --> 00:00:18,841
very front and one near the very end, and both of them are exactly the kind of

6
00:00:18,841 --> 00:00:21,145
decision a PM needs to have an opinion on.

7
00:00:21,145 --> 00:00:25,315
This lecture is the full map, so the next three lectures each have somewhere

8
00:00:25,315 --> 00:00:26,413
specific to plug in.

9
00:00:26,413 --> 00:00:30,638
Think of this the way an architect thinks of a floor plan versus a structural

10
00:00:30,638 --> 00:00:31,077
drawing.

11
00:00:31,077 --> 00:00:35,631
Section two gave you the floor plan, enough to understand how someone moves through

12
00:00:35,631 --> 00:00:36,179
the space.

13
00:00:36,179 --> 00:00:40,459
This lecture is closer to the structural drawing: every load-bearing stage, in

14
00:00:40,459 --> 00:00:43,971
order, so you know exactly where a crack would actually show up.

15
00:00:44,521 --> 00:00:48,879
Read down that right column and notice the pattern: the stages bracketing the

16
00:00:48,879 --> 00:00:51,709
pipeline, the first and the last three, are yours.

17
00:00:51,709 --> 00:00:55,841
The middle is mostly engineering's to build once you've made those calls.

18
00:00:55,841 --> 00:01:00,539
Ingestion is the one people skip discussing entirely, and it's the highest-leverage

19
00:01:00,539 --> 00:01:02,124
decision in the whole table.

20
00:01:02,124 --> 00:01:06,766
Every document you include is something that can be retrieved and shown to a user,

21
00:01:06,766 --> 00:01:11,464
and every document you exclude is a question the system will now confidently answer

22
00:01:11,464 --> 00:01:15,313
wrong instead of well, because it was never told that source exists.

23
00:01:15,313 --> 00:01:17,067
That's not an engineering call.

24
00:01:17,067 --> 00:01:21,596
It's a scope decision, and it belongs in your PRD's dataset section from Section

25
00:01:21,596 --> 00:01:21,935
three.

26
00:01:21,935 --> 00:01:24,992
Citation deserves the same attention at the other end.

27
00:01:24,992 --> 00:01:29,690
Showing a source next to an answer changes the entire trust relationship a user has

28
00:01:29,690 --> 00:01:30,652
with the feature.

29
00:01:30,652 --> 00:01:34,784
A cited answer invites verification, an uncited one asks for blind faith.

30
00:01:34,784 --> 00:01:39,256
That's a real product decision with real trade-offs, not a formatting detail an

31
00:01:39,256 --> 00:01:43,218
engineer bolts on at the end because someone remembered to ask for it.

32
00:01:43,768 --> 00:01:48,265
Here's the full sequence, laid out as one line instead of the two parallel paths

33
00:01:48,265 --> 00:01:49,220
from Section two.

34
00:01:49,220 --> 00:01:50,344
Both views are true.

35
00:01:50,344 --> 00:01:53,323
That one showed what runs once versus what runs live.

36
00:01:53,323 --> 00:01:57,427
This one shows the complete journey, start to end, for a single question.

37
00:01:57,427 --> 00:02:01,417
Re-ranking is the stage most PMs have never heard named, and it's worth

38
00:02:01,417 --> 00:02:02,991
understanding why it exists.

39
00:02:02,991 --> 00:02:07,713
Initial retrieval is built for speed, scanning thousands of chunks fast, which means

40
00:02:07,713 --> 00:02:12,434
it optimises for finding roughly the right neighbourhood, not necessarily the single

41
00:02:12,434 --> 00:02:13,109
best answer.

42
00:02:13,109 --> 00:02:17,661
Re-ranking takes that shortlist, maybe twenty candidates, and runs a slower, more

43
00:02:17,661 --> 00:02:22,046
careful pass to reorder them before only the top few actually reach the model.

44
00:02:22,046 --> 00:02:24,687
It's a precision step bolted onto a speed step.

45
00:02:24,687 --> 00:02:29,240
Picture it as the difference between a librarian pulling every book that mentions

46
00:02:29,240 --> 00:02:33,962
your topic, and then actually reading the first paragraph of each one before handing

47
00:02:33,962 --> 00:02:35,704
you the three that matter most.

48
00:02:35,704 --> 00:02:38,177
The first pass is fast and broad on purpose.

49
00:02:38,177 --> 00:02:40,763
The second pass is slow and narrow on purpose.

50
00:02:40,763 --> 00:02:44,641
Neither one alone does the whole job as well as both stages together.

51
00:02:45,191 --> 00:02:49,953
Say the honest caveat, because it's tempting to treat every stage on that table as

52
00:02:49,953 --> 00:02:52,102
something a good pipeline simply has.

53
00:02:52,102 --> 00:02:56,806
Re-ranking adds a real, measurable delay, an entire second pass over a shortlist,

54
00:02:56,806 --> 00:02:58,548
before generation even starts.

55
00:02:58,548 --> 00:03:03,252
For some features that delay is completely worth it, a legal or medical retrieval

56
00:03:03,252 --> 00:03:05,691
system where precision matters enormously.

57
00:03:05,691 --> 00:03:10,337
For a low-stakes internal tool, it can be latency spent on a quality improvement

58
00:03:10,337 --> 00:03:11,441
nobody will notice.

59
00:03:11,441 --> 00:03:16,261
Ask the same question latency and throughput taught you to ask about anything: does

60
00:03:16,261 --> 00:03:21,023
this stage earn its cost for this specific feature, or did it just sound like best

61
00:03:21,023 --> 00:03:21,546
practice?

62
00:03:21,546 --> 00:03:25,669
The same test applies to every stage on the table, not just re-ranking.

63
00:03:25,669 --> 00:03:30,315
A pipeline with all seven stages fully built isn't automatically better than one

64
00:03:30,315 --> 00:03:30,896
with five.

65
00:03:30,896 --> 00:03:35,483
It's appropriate when the feature's archetype and blast radius from Section one

66
00:03:35,483 --> 00:03:38,852
actually justify the extra latency and engineering effort.

67
00:03:38,852 --> 00:03:42,859
Building every optional stage by default is the RAG equivalent of the

68
00:03:42,859 --> 00:03:47,505
hundred-percent threshold from Section three: it sounds thorough and mostly just

69
00:03:47,505 --> 00:03:49,073
adds cost nobody asked for.

70
00:03:49,623 --> 00:03:54,162
That's the term worth having by name, because "should we add re-ranking" is now a

71
00:03:54,162 --> 00:03:58,870
specific, answerable question instead of a vague gesture at making retrieval better.

72
00:03:58,870 --> 00:04:03,578
The exercise is the same habit this whole course keeps returning to: name the actual

73
00:04:03,578 --> 00:04:07,837
stages, out loud, rather than trusting a mental picture of "it searches some

74
00:04:07,837 --> 00:04:12,545
documents." Most people find at least one stage in that table nobody on the team has

75
00:04:12,545 --> 00:04:13,610
actually discussed.

76
00:04:13,610 --> 00:04:18,317
Do this before your next conversation with engineering about the feature, not during

77
00:04:18,317 --> 00:04:18,486
it.

78
00:04:18,486 --> 00:04:22,745
Walking in already able to name all seven stages, and which ones your system

79
00:04:22,745 --> 00:04:27,172
actually has, changes that conversation from you asking to be educated into you

80
00:04:27,172 --> 00:04:29,190
confirming what you already suspect.

81
00:04:29,740 --> 00:04:31,463
One thing before you move on.

82
00:04:31,463 --> 00:04:34,553
Map your feature against this table, stage by stage.

83
00:04:34,553 --> 00:04:39,544
Most real pipelines turn out to be missing at least one row, usually ingestion scope

84
00:04:39,544 --> 00:04:44,475
or citation, not because anyone decided against them, but because nobody decided at

85
00:04:44,475 --> 00:04:44,713
all.

86
00:04:44,713 --> 00:04:47,743
Find that undecided stage and name it specifically.

87
00:04:47,743 --> 00:04:52,734
It's exactly the kind of gap that looks fine in a demo and breaks the moment a real,

88
00:04:52,734 --> 00:04:57,309
awkward question arrives, usually in front of the one user you most wanted to

89
00:04:57,309 --> 00:04:57,785
impress.

90
00:04:57,785 --> 00:05:02,657
Next lecture goes deep on the stage most PMs assume is purely technical and isn't:

91
00:05:02,657 --> 00:05:06,875
chunking, and the decisions inside it that are genuinely yours to make.
